<BaseLayout title="Product Thinking" description="How Rishav approaches AI product discovery, prioritization, architecture, evaluation, and honest evidence.">
  <header class="page-hero shell"><p class="eyebrow">My operating system for AI products</p><h1 class="display">A model can generate. A product must <em>decide, recover and earn trust.</em></h1><p class="lead">My work connects product discovery to system design. The point is not to maximize the number of AI features—it is to find the smallest reliable behavior that improves a real decision.</p></header>
  <section class="section shell thinking-grid"><article><span>01</span><h2>Problem framing</h2><p>Define the user, job, current workaround, decision cost, and missing evidence before selecting a model.</p></article><article><span>02</span><h2>Risk-first MVP</h2><p>Scope a vertical slice that tests value, permissions, failure recovery, and measurement together.</p></article><article><span>03</span><h2>Architecture as product</h2><p>Make data, identity, retrieval, orchestration, tools, humans, latency, cost, and observability visible.</p></article><article><span>04</span><h2>Evaluation loop</h2><p>Build golden datasets, slice failures, separate deterministic checks from subjective judges, and gate releases.</p></article></section>
  <section class="section approach-section"><div class="shell"><p class="eyebrow">Prioritization lens</p><div class="decision-matrix"><div class="matrix-axis y">Evidence strength →</div><div class="matrix-axis x">User value →</div><article class="m1"><strong>Prototype</strong><span>High learning, bounded cost</span></article><article class="m2"><strong>Build</strong><span>Strong value and evidence</span></article><article class="m3"><strong>Research</strong><span>Interesting, not yet justified</span></article><article class="m4"><strong>Reject</strong><span>AI adds complexity without value</span></article></div></div></section>
  <section class="section shell"><div class="section-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Research traceability</p><h2>Recommendation versus adaptation.</h2></div><p>Aishwarya Srinivasan’s public project guidance informed the portfolio categories. Product names, interfaces, architectures, evaluation plans, and technology choices here are my adaptations unless explicitly described otherwise.</p></div><div class="source-list">{researchSources.map((source)=><a href={source.url}><span>Public source</span><strong>{source.label}</strong><em>↗</em></a>)}</div><p class="disclosure">No endorsement, affiliation, or course participation is claimed. External facts are linked to their public source; hypothetical targets remain labeled.</p></section>
</BaseLayout>

